section.para:
			The example below embeds a minimal 5x7 PNG as a data URI:

String text = "An image data:image/png;base64,"
    + "iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAUAAAAHCAYAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA"
    + " follows.";

AssetDetector detector = new CursorAssetDetector();
EmbeddedAsset asset = detector.detect(text).get(0);
// asset.format()        = "png"
// asset.mediaType()     = "image/png"
// asset.width()         = 5
// asset.height()        = 7
// asset.decodedLength() = 45
byte[] bytes = asset.decode(text);  // the original 45 bytes

			The asset's span covers the whole URI; its payload span covers just the
			base64 characters, and decode reads them straight from the
			original text.
			AssetsManualExampleTest asserts the behavior shown here.

			Detection needs no data: URI: the same payload pasted bare into
			prose is found by its magic prefix and verified against its decoded header:

String bare = "A report pastes "
    + "iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAUAAAAHCAYAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA"
    + " into the body.";

EmbeddedAsset found = new CursorAssetDetector().detect(bare).get(0);
// found.format()        = "png", sniffed from the decoded header
// found.mediaType()     = "image/png", derived from the format
// found.width()         = 5
// found.height()        = 7
// found.decodedLength() = 45

			A bare run carries no URI around its payload, so the asset's span and its
			payload span cover the same characters.
			AssetsManualExampleTest asserts the behavior shown here.

			Compact JSON Web Tokens from
			RFC 7519
			are recognized as three base64url segments. The header and claims must decode
			as UTF-8 JSON objects, the header must carry a non-empty string-valued
			alg, and each segment is bounded to 16 KiB. For an unsecured token,
			alg must be none and the signature segment must be empty;
			all other algorithms require a non-empty signature segment. The full token is
			the asset span and the claims segment is the payload:

String jwt = "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9"
    + ".eyJzdWIiOiIxMjMifQ.AAAA";

EmbeddedAsset token = new CursorAssetDetector().detect(jwt).get(0);
// token.format()    = "jwt"
// token.mediaType() = "application/jwt"
// new String(token.decode(jwt), StandardCharsets.UTF_8) = "{\"sub\":\"123\"}"

			Detection validates structure only. It does not verify a JWT signature, trust a
			key, or make an authorization decision. AssetsManualExampleTest
			asserts the example exactly.
